(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-pericardial-disease-vignettes"]={"title":"Pericardial Disease Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 45-year-old woman taking warfarin for a mechanical valve presents with 2 days of sharp chest pain eased by leaning forward, widespread ST elevation and a small effusion. She is afebrile and her blood pressure is 128/78. Which is the most appropriate disposition?","choices":["Discharge with clinic follow-up","Discharge after one dose of ibuprofen","Outpatient cardiac imaging next week","Admit her to the hospital"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. She has typical acute pericarditis, but warfarin places her among patients on oral anticoagulants, a feature associated with higher risk and a potential need for hospitalization. She is afebrile and stable, yet the anticoagulation by itself favors admission. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has heart-failure-like edema, ascites and an elevated jugular venous pressure. The differential is narrowed to constrictive pericarditis or restrictive cardiomyopathy. Which laboratory finding would favor constrictive pericarditis?","choices":["A markedly high natriuretic peptide","A normal sedimentation rate","Positive blood cultures","A relatively low natriuretic peptide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. No B-type natriuretic peptide threshold confirms or excludes constriction, but the level is likely lower in constrictive pericarditis than in restrictive cardiomyopathy. With the differential narrowed to those two, a relatively low value tips toward constriction. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man with typical acute pericarditis after a viral illness has a troponin that is above the reference range. Coronary angiography shows normal arteries. Which process does the troponin elevation most likely reflect?","choices":["A false-positive laboratory result","Myocardial involvement (myopericarditis)","An unrecognized coronary occlusion","Developing constrictive physiology"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Typical acute pericarditis after a viral illness with a troponin above the reference range and normal coronary arteries means the inflammation has reached the myocardium, myopericarditis. Troponin may rise when the myocardium is also affected, and an elevated troponin is a higher-risk feature that can call for hospitalization. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 32-year-old man has typical acute viral pericarditis with stable vital signs and no high-risk features. A transthoracic echocardiogram is ordered. Which result is most expected?","choices":["A normal study or only a small effusion","Right ventricular diastolic collapse","A thick pericardium with septal bounce","Apical ballooning with a normal base"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In typical acute viral pericarditis with stable vital signs and no high-risk features, the echocardiogram is often normal unless there is an associated effusion, so a normal study or a small effusion is expected. The chest radiograph is typically normal too, and most such patients need no invasive workup. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman develops fever and positional chest pain with a friction rub (a scratchy, squeaking sound) 2 weeks after a myocardial infarction treated with a coronary stent. Echocardiography shows a small effusion and her vital signs are stable. Which anti-inflammatory regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Aspirin plus colchicine","Indomethacin plus colchicine","Prednisone plus colchicine","Ibuprofen plus colchicine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fever, positional pain and a rub 2 weeks after a stented myocardial infarction make this post-infarction pericarditis. Nonsteroidal drugs other than aspirin, such as ibuprofen and indomethacin, are not given for post-infarction pericarditis, and glucocorticoids are also avoided, so aspirin, itself a first-line option, is paired with colchicine. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man with acute pericarditis says the pain keeps him awake because it is worse when he lies down. Which advice is most appropriate?","choices":["Sleep prone to splint the chest","Take an extra dose at bedtime","Keep the head of the bed elevated","Sleep flat to rest the heart"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pericardial pain is worse when supine, which is why lying down keeps him awake, and keeping the head of the bed elevated is part of follow-up counseling. That advice sits with adherence to the full treatment course, activity restriction and recognizing red-flag symptoms. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with a new inferior myocardial infarction involving the right ventricle is hypotensive with distended jugular veins and clear lungs. Tamponade is considered. Why must his infarction stay on the differential?","choices":["It excludes tamponade completely","It mimics subacute tamponade instead","It is the commonest cause","It mimics acute tamponade"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hypotension with distended jugular veins and clear lungs fits tamponade, but a right ventricular infarction can look the same. Myocardial infarction, especially with right ventricular involvement, heads the differential of acute tamponade along with aortic dissection and massive pulmonary embolism, so his infarction must stay on the list. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman with metastatic lung cancer has 2 days of worsening dyspnea and chest discomfort. Blood pressure is 84/56, heart rate 122, jugular veins are distended, and heart sounds are muffled. Her blood pressure drops by 18 mmHg on inspiration (pulsus paradoxus, an inspiratory drop in blood pressure). Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cardiac tamponade","Constrictive pericarditis","Acute pericarditis","Tension pneumothorax"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Blood pressure 84/56 with distended jugular veins and muffled heart sounds is the Beck triad, and an 18 mmHg inspiratory drop is pulsus paradoxus. Malignancy is a listed cause of pericardial effusion, and over 2 days even a small effusion can compress the heart, since acute effusions can cause tamponade with under 200 mL of fluid. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man began having pleuritic (worse with a deep breath) pericardial pain 8 weeks ago. Despite treatment the symptoms have never fully gone away, with no symptom-free interval. Which term best describes his course?","choices":["Chronic pericarditis","Effusive-constrictive disease","Incessant pericarditis","Recurrent pericarditis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. His symptoms have persisted for 8 weeks, beyond the 4 to 6 weeks of acute pericarditis but short of 3 months, with no symptom-free interval. Symptoms persisting more than 4 to 6 weeks and up to 3 months without a sustained remission define incessant pericarditis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man received chest radiation for lymphoma 20 years ago. He has 8 months of exertional dyspnea, fatigue, leg edema and ascites. His jugular venous pressure is elevated and shows the Kussmaul sign (it rises on inspiration instead of falling), and a pericardial knock (an early diastolic sound, slightly earlier than a third heart sound) is heard. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute pericarditis","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Infective endocarditis","Constrictive pericarditis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Eight months of fluid overload (edema, ascites) and diminished cardiac output (fatigue, exertional dyspnea), an elevated jugular venous pressure with the Kussmaul sign and a pericardial knock fit constrictive pericarditis. Prior chest radiation is a listed cause. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with a large pericardial effusion is hypotensive and tachycardic. His electrocardiogram shows small QRS complexes with low voltage (small amplitude complexes) whose height alternates from beat to beat. Which electrocardiogram finding is this?","choices":["Second-degree heart block","Diffuse PR depression","Electrical alternans","Ventricular bigeminy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. QRS complexes whose height alternates from beat to beat are electrical alternans, fairly specific for cardiac tamponade, though rarely seen with very large effusions. With low voltage, hypotension and tachycardia in a man with a large effusion, it supports tamponade, and sinus tachycardia here is an indication for immediate pericardiocentesis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man becomes hypotensive and short of breath an hour after a catheter procedure perforates his heart. Echocardiogram shows only a small pericardial effusion, estimated at about 150 mL, with right ventricular diastolic collapse. Which best explains why so little fluid has compromised him?","choices":["Chronic stretching of the sac","The echocardiogram underestimated a liter","The pericardium was already calcified","Rapid filling of an unstretched sac"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. His effusion formed within an hour of the perforation, and acute pericardial effusions can cause hemodynamic compromise with small amounts, under 200 mL. Chronic effusions can grow large without tamponade because the pericardium stretches over time; a sudden 150 mL gives the sac no time to stretch. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man with acute pericarditis has taken ibuprofen and colchicine for 2 weeks. He has been symptom-free for 4 days, and his weekly C-reactive protein has just returned to normal. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Stop every medication today","Increase the anti-inflammatory","Switch over to prednisone","Begin tapering the anti-inflammatory"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In the C-reactive protein approach, tapering starts once the patient is symptom-free and the C-reactive protein has normalized, and he now meets both conditions. The anti-inflammatory drug is tapered while colchicine continues for its usual course of about 3 months after a first episode. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman is being treated for acute pericarditis with an anti-inflammatory and colchicine. She asks whether she may take brisk walks while recovering. Which heart rate ceiling should guide her activity?","choices":["Under 60 beats per minute","Under 100 beats per minute","Under 150 beats per minute","No limit if she feels well"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Strenuous physical activity is avoided until her symptoms resolve and C-reactive protein normalizes, for at least 1 month after diagnosis, and activity is kept to a maximal heart rate under 100 beats per minute. Any walking during recovery should stay within that ceiling. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with end-stage kidney disease has a large pericardial effusion with stable blood pressure, and his blood urea nitrogen is 112 mg/dL. Which treatment addresses the cause?","choices":["Antituberculous therapy","Corticosteroids","Pericardiectomy","Dialysis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. End-stage kidney disease with a blood urea nitrogen of 112 mg/dL points to a uremic effusion, and treatment of pericardial disease includes treating any underlying disorder. For uremia, that means dialysis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman is discharged on ibuprofen and colchicine for acute pericarditis. Which new symptom should prompt her to go straight to the emergency department rather than call for an appointment?","choices":["Lightheadedness with shortness of breath","Mild fatigue in the late afternoon","Pain that still eases on leaning forward","Mild indigestion after doses of ibuprofen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lightheadedness with shortness of breath suggests the falling blood pressure of tamponade, one of the red-flag symptoms patients are taught to recognize at discharge. A possible tamponade needs emergency evaluation, not a scheduled appointment. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with known malignant pericardial effusion has a blood pressure of 82/50, distended neck veins, muffled heart sounds and pulsus paradoxus (a 16 mmHg drop in blood pressure with inspiration). Echocardiogram shows a large effusion with right ventricular diastolic collapse. Which is the definitive treatment?","choices":["Loop diuretic infusion","Expedient pericardiocentesis","Intravenous fluids alone","High-dose anti-inflammatories"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hypotension, distended neck veins, muffled heart sounds and a 16 mmHg pulsus paradoxus with right ventricular diastolic collapse are tamponade from his malignant effusion. Expedient pericardiocentesis is the definitive treatment, and the drained fluid can be sent for diagnostic studies such as cytology. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man has long-standing constrictive pericarditis with marked edema and ascites despite diuretics. A computed tomography scan shows a thick, heavily calcified pericardium. Which treatment offers definitive relief?","choices":["Pericardiectomy","Pericardiocentesis","High-dose colchicine","Heart transplantation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Long-standing constriction with edema and ascites despite diuretics and a thick, heavily calcified pericardium is established disease, and constrictive pericarditis is often a surgical disease. Pericardiectomy, removal of part or most of the pericardium, gives a 5-year survival of nearly 80% and a 10-year survival of nearly 60%. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with chronic kidney disease, pulmonary hypertension and hyponatremia is diagnosed with constrictive pericarditis. His family asks what to expect. Which statement about his long-term outlook is most accurate?","choices":["Age does not influence the outcome of surgery","His prognosis is as good as a young patient's","Survival without treatment is usually normal","His age and comorbidities predict a worse outcome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Older age, worse functional class and comorbidities such as renal dysfunction, pulmonary hypertension and hyponatremia are independent predictors of poor long-term outcome in constrictive pericarditis. At 78 with kidney disease, pulmonary hypertension and hyponatremia, he carries several of them. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 56-year-old woman has fatigue, weight gain, cold intolerance, dry skin and a heart rate of 54. Echocardiogram shows a large pericardial effusion without chamber collapse. Which test best addresses the cause her features suggest?","choices":["Tuberculin skin test","Blood cultures","Thyroid function tests","Antinuclear antibody"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fatigue, weight gain, cold intolerance, dry skin and a heart rate of 54 suggest myxedema (hypothyroidism), a metabolic cause of effusion. Thyroid function tests sit in the effusion lab workup specifically for myxedema, and the underlying cause is usually found through a careful history and physical examination, exactly as her features show. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man has a moderate-to-large pericardial effusion on echocardiogram with no hemodynamic compromise. Viral, uremic, thyroid and rheumatologic workups are unrevealing. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Immediate pericardiectomy","Diagnostic pericardiocentesis","Reassure and discharge","Start empiric anakinra"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A moderate-to-large effusion whose viral, uremic, thyroid and rheumatologic workups are unrevealing is an effusion of unknown etiology, an indication for diagnostic pericardiocentesis. The fluid is sent for cell count, Gram stain, bacterial and fungal culture, acid-fast stain with mycobacterial culture, and cytology. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has her second recurrence of idiopathic pericarditis despite colchicine. Her temperature is 38.4 degrees Celsius, C-reactive protein is 45 mg/L, and cardiac magnetic resonance shows pericardial edema with late gadolinium enhancement. Which therapy is most appropriate to add?","choices":["Azathioprine","Pericardiectomy","Anakinra","Intravenous immunoglobulin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A second recurrence of idiopathic pericarditis despite colchicine, with a temperature of 38.4 degrees Celsius, a C-reactive protein of 45 mg/L (above 10) and pericardial inflammation on magnetic resonance, is the inflammatory phenotype. An interleukin-1 blocker such as anakinra or rilonacept is added and is preferred for colchicine-resistant recurrence. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman has 3 days of sharp substernal chest pain that improves when she sits forward. Her vital signs are normal, and no rub is heard on careful auscultation. Which additional finding would complete the clinical diagnosis of acute pericarditis?","choices":["Widespread ST elevation on electrocardiogram","A troponin above the reference range","An elevated B-type natriuretic peptide","Pericardial calcification on radiograph"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acute pericarditis needs two of four criteria: sharp substernal chest pain, a pericardial friction rub, suggestive electrocardiogram changes (widespread ST elevation) and a new or worsening effusion. She has the pain, sharp and eased by sitting forward, but no rub, so widespread ST elevation would supply the second criterion. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 14, 19, 26"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with suspected tamponade has distended jugular veins and a blood pressure of 88/60. He has leg edema, and drainage is being arranged. Which drug class should be avoided?","choices":["Local anesthetics","Antibiotics","Diuretics","Intravenous crystalloid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In tamponade, intravenous fluids are given to increase preload and prevent right ventricular collapse while drainage is arranged, so a diuretic, which works against that goal, should be avoided despite his leg edema. Expedient pericardiocentesis is the definitive treatment. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with a large pericardial effusion from metastatic cancer reports a hoarse voice, hiccups and difficulty swallowing. Which mechanism explains his hoarseness?","choices":["Superior vena cava obstruction","Recurrent laryngeal nerve impingement","Direct invasion of the vocal cords","Hypoglossal nerve compression"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. His effusion is large, and large effusions can cause dysphagia, nausea, hiccups, cough and hoarseness. The hoarseness comes from impingement of the recurrent laryngeal nerve, which is why his voice change travels with the hiccups and difficulty swallowing. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman developed early signs of constriction 6 weeks after an episode of acute pericarditis. She is hemodynamically stable, and cardiac magnetic resonance shows pericardial edema with late gadolinium enhancement. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Urgent pericardiocentesis","Observation with no treatment","A longer course of pericarditis drugs","Pericardiectomy within the week"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Her constriction is early and she is hemodynamically stable, and pericardial edema with late gadolinium enhancement on cardiac magnetic resonance marks inflammatory, potentially reversible constriction. Such patients may get a trial of the acute pericarditis medicines, nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and colchicine, for a longer 2 to 3 months, with diuretics for fluid overload. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 31, 49"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has acute pericarditis with a friction rub (a scratchy, squeaking sound) and widespread ST elevation. Her clinician wants laboratory values that confirm inflammation and can guide the length of therapy. Which pair of tests is most appropriate?","choices":["C-reactive protein and sedimentation rate","D-dimer and B-type natriuretic peptide","Lipase and hepatic transaminases","Blood cultures and procalcitonin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein are the inflammatory markers in the initial laboratory workup of acute pericarditis, alongside a complete blood count and troponins. With her rub and widespread ST elevation confirming the diagnosis, they are the pair that measures the inflammation itself. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with ovarian cancer has had three drainages for a recurrent pericardial effusion, and the latest echocardiogram shows a loculated collection behind the heart. Which procedure is most appropriate?","choices":["Diuretic therapy","Pericardial window","Fourth needle tap","Thoracentesis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. She has had three drainages for a recurrent effusion and now has a loculated collection behind the heart, two separate indications for a pericardial window or pericardiectomy. A posterior loculated effusion cannot be approached percutaneously, and the window also addresses the recurrence. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman with confirmed constrictive pericarditis has failed a 3-month trial of anti-inflammatories and diuretics and remains fluid overloaded. Which service performs the definitive procedure she needs?","choices":["Nephrology","Cardiothoracic surgery","Interventional radiology","Gastroenterology"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Her confirmed constriction has failed a 3-month medical trial of anti-inflammatories and diuretics and she remains fluid overloaded, so the next step is a definitive operation rather than more medication. Cardiothoracic surgery, working alongside cardiology, is the service listed for pericardial disease and performs that procedure. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman is diagnosed with constrictive pericarditis. She had mantle-field radiotherapy for Hodgkin lymphoma 25 years ago and is otherwise well. Which feature of her history most worsens her long-term prognosis?","choices":["Her prior chest radiation","Her female sex","Her lack of other illness","Her cured lymphoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Etiology is an important determinant of survival in constrictive pericarditis, and prior ionizing radiation is associated with the worst survival rates. Her mantle-field radiotherapy 25 years ago is therefore the feature that most worsens her outlook. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 50"}]};
